\title{The Attention-Budget Knee:\\ A Summability Criterion for Context-Stable Key Budgets}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{23 August 2026}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A transformer attends to a context of $n$ tokens through a probability vector over keys. Keeping only the $k$ heaviest keys retains a fraction $R_w(n,k)$ of the attention mass; fixing a gate $\tau$, the smallest budget clearing the bar is the \emph{knee} $k^*(n)$. Empirically the knee chain rises along a context ladder for one model ($\{16,20,24\}$) and is flat for a larger one ($\{16,16\}$), and a fine sweep at context length $1024$ shows every budget below $16$ failing (retained masses $0.9318$, $0.9532$, $0.9660$, $0.9759$ at $k=4,6,8,12$) while $16$ passes. We develop a model-free theory of this object. Monotonicity alone converts a fail/pass pair into the bracket $a < k^*(n) \le b$, giving $12 < k^*(1024) \le 16$. Geometric decay $w_{i+1}\le r w_i$ yields a single budget $K(r,\tau)=\max\{\lceil \log((1-\tau)(1-r))/\log r\rceil,1\}$ valid at every context length, while a positive uniform floor forces $k^*(n)\ge \tau nc/M$ and unbounded context sensitivity, so the two regimes genuinely separate. Exact flatness $k^*(2n)=k^*(n)$ is false even for $w_i=2^{-i}$, so a two-point measurement can only support uniform boundedness. The exact criterion is classical: for every interior gate, a context-independent budget exists if and only if the sorted profile is summable; on Zipf profiles $(i+1)^{-s}$ this gives a phase transition at the critical exponent $s=1$. Finally, merged heads obey a mediant law, so knees sandwich and stability is closed under mixing but destroyed by a single gapless head.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The measured object}

Each attention head at each position produces a probability distribution over the $n$ keys in its context. Sparsification methods---top-$k$ attention, key--value cache eviction, streaming attention---rest on the observation that this distribution is heavily concentrated. The quantitative question is how concentrated, and whether concentration survives growth of the context.

Write the head's attention weights in decreasing order as $w_0 \ge w_1 \ge \cdots > 0$. Truncating to the top $k$ retains
\[
R_w(n,k) \;=\; \frac{\sum_{i<\min(k,n)} w_i}{\sum_{i<n} w_i}.
\]
Fix a retention gate $\tau \in (0,1)$ and define the \emph{knee} $k^*(n) = \min\{k : R_w(n,k) \ge \tau\}$, the smallest budget preserving a $\tau$-fraction of the mass, and the \emph{context sensitivity} $\Delta(n) = k^*(2n) - k^*(n)$.

\subsection{The empirical puzzle}

At gate $\tau = 0.98$, a ladder of context lengths gives a rising chain $\{16,20,24\}$ for a $0.5$-billion-parameter model and a flat chain $\{16,16\}$ for a $1.5$-billion-parameter model. A fine sweep of the larger model at context length $1024$ measures retained masses $0.9318$, $0.9532$, $0.9660$, $0.9759$ at $k = 4,6,8,12$, all below the gate, with $k=16$ passing; the value at $k=12$ misses by roughly two standard errors. Thus the knee does not decline with scale. Two questions follow: what do such grids logically establish, and what property of a model makes its chain flat? We answer both, and the answer to the second is not parameter count.

Throughout, no probabilistic model of language, no assumption about corpora, and no architectural hypothesis is used; positivity of the weights suffices for most statements, and decay conditions are stated explicitly where needed.

\section{Definitions and elementary theory}

Let $w:\N \to \R$ be an \emph{attention profile}, indexed in retention order; all results assume $w_i > 0$.

\begin{definition}[Head mass] $H_w(k) = \sum_{i=0}^{k-1} w_i$, with $H_w(0)=0$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Retained mass] $R_w(n,k) = H_w(\min(k,n))/H_w(n)$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Knee] $k^*_w(n,\tau) = \inf\{k \in \N : \tau \le R_w(n,k)\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Context sensitivity] $\Delta_w(n,\tau) = k^*_w(2n,\tau) - k^*_w(n,\tau)$, truncated subtraction on $\N$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Context stability] $w$ is \emph{context stable} at $\tau$ if there is $K \in \N$ with $k^*_w(n,\tau) \le K$ for all $n \ge 1$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Monotonicity of head mass]\label{lem:hm}
$H_w$ is strictly increasing, and $H_w(n) > 0$ for $n \ge 1$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
$H_w(b)-H_w(a) = \sum_{a\le i<b} w_i > 0$ for $a<b$: each summand is positive and the index set nonempty.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Retained mass]\label{lem:ret}
For $n \ge 1$: $0 \le R_w(n,k) \le 1$ and $R_w(n,n)=1$; $k \mapsto R_w(n,k)$ is non-decreasing; $R_w(n,k) < 1$ strictly for $k<n$; and $R_w(n,a) < R_w(n,b)$ whenever $a<b$ and $a<n$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The denominator is a positive constant in $k$, so all claims reduce to Lemma~\ref{lem:hm} applied to $H_w(\min(k,n))$, using $\min(a,n) < \min(b,n)$ when $a<b$ and $a<n$.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[No plateau below the knee]\label{prop:plateau}
For every positive profile and every $n > 12$,
\[
R_w(n,4) < R_w(n,6) < R_w(n,8) < R_w(n,12).
\]
\end{proposition}

Hence the measured chain $0.9318 < 0.9532 < 0.9660 < 0.9759$ is not a corpus accident: strict increase along a sub-knee grid is forced by positivity alone, so each failing grid point carries independent information.

\begin{lemma}[Attainability]\label{lem:attain}
If $n \ge 1$ and $\tau \le 1$, the gate set $\{k : \tau \le R_w(n,k)\}$ contains $n$; hence the knee is well defined, $\tau \le R_w(n,k^*)$, and $k^*(n) \le n$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Context dilution]\label{lem:dilute}
For fixed $k$ and $1 \le n_1 \le n_2$, $R_w(n_2,k) \le R_w(n_1,k)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
If $k \ge n_1$ then $R_w(n_1,k)=1$. Otherwise $\min(k,n_1)=\min(k,n_2)=k$, so the numerators agree while the denominator only grows.
\end{proof}

\section{The razor: what two measurements prove}

\begin{theorem}[Razor bracket]\label{thm:razor}
Let $w$ be positive, $n \ge 1$, $\tau \le 1$. If $R_w(n,a) < \tau$ and $\tau \le R_w(n,b)$, then
\[
a < k^*_w(n,\tau) \le b .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$b$ lies in the gate set, so $k^* \le b$. If $k^* \le a$, monotonicity (Lemma~\ref{lem:ret}) gives $\tau \le R_w(n,k^*) \le R_w(n,a) < \tau$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

Only monotonicity is used: no distributional assumption, no sampling model, no architectural hypothesis. The bracket is a deduction, not a statistical inference.

\begin{corollary}[The measured bracket]\label{cor:measured}
If $R_w(n,12) = 0.9759$ and $0.98 \le R_w(n,16)$ for a positive profile at $n \ge 1$, then $12 < k^*_w(n,0.98) \le 16$.
\end{corollary}

This is the honest content of ``the knee is exactly $16$''. Together with the refuted points $k=4,6,8$, no sub-$16$ budget on the tested grid passes; the residual ambiguity is the grid's, not the mathematics'.

\begin{lemma}[Gate monotonicity]
If $\tau_1 \le \tau_2 \le 1$ then $k^*(n,\tau_1) \le k^*(n,\tau_2)$.
\end{lemma}

\section{Regime I: geometric decay gives a universal budget}

\begin{definition}
$w$ has \emph{decay ratio} $r$ if $w_{i+1} \le r\,w_i$ for all $i$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Geometric domination]\label{lem:dom}
If $r \ge 0$ and $w$ has decay ratio $r$, then $w_i \le w_0 r^i$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{lemma}[Tail estimate]\label{lem:tail}
If $0<r<1$ and $w$ has decay ratio $r$, then for $k \le n$,
\[
H_w(n)-H_w(k) = \sum_{k \le i < n} w_i \le \frac{w_0 r^k}{1-r}.
\]
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
Bound each term by $w_0 r^i$ (Lemma~\ref{lem:dom}) and sum: $\sum_{k\le i<n} r^i = (r^k-r^n)/(1-r) \le r^k/(1-r)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Uniform mass guarantee]\label{thm:uniformmass}
Let $w$ be positive with decay ratio $r \in (0,1)$. For all $k \ge 1$ and all $n \ge 1$,
\[
R_w(n,k) \;\ge\; 1 - \frac{r^k}{1-r},
\]
a bound independent of the context length.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
If $n \le k$ then $R_w(n,k)=1$. Otherwise $\min(k,n)=k$ and
\[
1 - R_w(n,k) = \frac{H_w(n)-H_w(k)}{H_w(n)} \le \frac{w_0 r^k/(1-r)}{H_w(n)} \le \frac{r^k}{1-r},
\]
by Lemma~\ref{lem:tail} and $H_w(n) \ge H_w(k) \ge H_w(1) = w_0$.
\end{proof}

The largest weight cancels between the discarded tail and the retained head; this is exactly why the guarantee is free of $n$.

\begin{theorem}[Context-stable regime]\label{thm:stable}
If $w$ is positive with decay ratio $r \in (0,1)$ and $\tau < 1$, then $w$ is context stable at $\tau$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Choose $K_0$ with $r^{K_0} < (1-\tau)(1-r)$ and set $K = \max(K_0,1)$. Then $r^K/(1-r) < 1-\tau$, so $\tau < 1 - r^K/(1-r) \le R_w(n,K)$ for all $n \ge 1$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:uniformmass}; hence $k^*(n) \le K$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Closed-form universal budget]\label{thm:closedform}
For positive $w$ with decay ratio $r \in (0,1)$ and gate $\tau<1$, put
\[
K(r,\tau) = \max\left\{ \left\lceil \frac{\log\big((1-\tau)(1-r)\big)}{\log r} \right\rceil,\ 1 \right\}.
\]
Then $k^*_w(n,\tau) \le K(r,\tau)$ for every $n \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Since $\log r < 0$, the ceiling bound $\log((1-\tau)(1-r))/\log r \le K$ rearranges to $K\log r \le \log((1-\tau)(1-r))$, i.e.\ $r^K \le (1-\tau)(1-r)$, i.e.\ $r^K/(1-r) \le 1-\tau$. Theorem~\ref{thm:uniformmass} gives $\tau \le R_w(n,K)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Under the hypotheses of Theorem~\ref{thm:stable}, $\Delta_w(n,\tau) \le K$ for all $n \ge 1$.
\end{corollary}

Read backwards, Theorem~\ref{thm:closedform} is an instrument: an observed universal budget $K$ constrains the decay ratio through $r^K \le (1-\tau)(1-r)$.

\section{Regime II: no spectral gap forces a linear budget}

\begin{theorem}[Linear lower bound]\label{thm:linear}
Let $w$ be positive with $c \le w_i \le M$ and $c>0$. For $n \ge 1$ and $\tau \le 1$,
\[
k^*_w(n,\tau) \;\ge\; \frac{\tau n c}{M}.
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Put $k = k^*(n,\tau)$, so $\tau H_w(n) \le H_w(\min(k,n)) \le kM$; also $H_w(n) \ge nc$. Hence $\tau n c \le kM$. (For $\tau \le 0$ the claim is trivial.)
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[The flat profile]\label{prop:flat}
For $w \equiv 1$, $R_w(n,k) = \min(k,n)/n$, and for $n\ge1$, $0<\tau\le1$,
\[
\tau n \le k^*_w(n,\tau) \le \lceil \tau n\rceil .
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Unbounded context sensitivity]\label{thm:unbounded}
For $w \equiv 1$ and $0<\tau\le1$, for every $K$ there is $n\ge1$ with $\Delta_w(n,\tau) > K$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Proposition~\ref{prop:flat}, $\Delta(n) \ge 2\tau n - (\tau n + 1) = \tau n - 1$; take $n > (K+3)/\tau$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}\label{cor:flatunstable}
The flat profile is context stable at no gate $\tau \in (0,1]$.
\end{corollary}

\section{The separation theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Context-sensitivity dichotomy]
Let $0<\tau<1$. The geometric profile $w_i = 2^{-i}$ admits a budget $K$ with $k^*(n)\le K$ for all $n\ge1$, while the flat profile $w\equiv1$ has $\sup_n \Delta(n) = \infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:stable} with $r=1/2$, and Theorem~\ref{thm:unbounded}.
\end{proof}

Whether a knee chain is flat or rising is thus a property of the decay profile of the sorted weights, and both behaviours are realisable. Nothing in the argument mentions parameter count: a flatter chain for a larger model means its sorted spectrum decays faster, a checkable internal statement not implied by size.

\section{Exact flatness is false}

\begin{theorem}[Refutation of exact flatness]\label{thm:flatfalse}
For $w_i = 2^{-i}$ at gate $\tau = 3/4$,
\[
k^*(1) = 1, \qquad k^*(2) = 2 .
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At $n=1$: $R(1,0)=0<3/4 \le 1 = R(1,1)$, so Theorem~\ref{thm:razor} brackets $0<k^*\le1$. At $n=2$: total mass $3/2$, so $R(2,1)=2/3<3/4$ and $R(2,2)=1$, bracketing $1<k^*\le2$.
\end{proof}

The mechanism is Lemma~\ref{lem:dilute}: the normaliser creeps upward with $n$, so the retained fraction of a fixed budget is weakly decreasing in $n$ and can cross the gate downwards. Exact equality of knees is a coincidence of gate placement, not a law.

\begin{remark}
A two-point measurement can support \emph{uniform boundedness} of the budget, never an equality. The right invariant is context stability, and the right report of a single measurement is a bracket.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}\label{prop:stableiff}
For positive $w$ and $\tau \le 1$, $w$ is context stable at $\tau$ iff there is a single $K$ with $\tau \le R_w(n,K)$ for all $n \ge 1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) $\tau \le R_w(n,k^*(n)) \le R_w(n,K)$ by monotonicity. ($\Leftarrow$) Immediate from the definition of the infimum.
\end{proof}

\section{The exact criterion: stability equals summability}

\begin{theorem}[Summability criterion]\label{thm:summable}
Let $w$ be positive and $0<\tau<1$. Then $w$ is context stable at $\tau$ if and only if $\sum_{i=0}^\infty w_i < \infty$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Rightarrow$) Suppose $K$ works at every $n$ but the series diverges; since the terms are positive, $H_w(n) \to \infty$. Choose $n \ge 1$ with $H_w(n) > H_w(K)/\tau$. Stability gives $\tau \le R_w(n,K) \le H_w(K)/H_w(n)$, i.e.\ $H_w(n) \le H_w(K)/\tau$, a contradiction. So the partial sums are bounded, and a positive series with bounded partial sums converges.

($\Leftarrow$) Let $S = \sum_i w_i < \infty$, so $S \ge w_0 > 0$ and $H_w(m) \le S$ for all $m$. Since $\tau S < S$ and $H_w(m) \to S$, pick $k$ with $\tau S < H_w(k)$. If $n \le k$ then $R_w(n,k) = 1 \ge \tau$; if $n>k$ then $\tau H_w(n) \le \tau S < H_w(k) = H_w(\min(k,n))$. Apply Proposition~\ref{prop:stableiff}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Gate independence]
If $w$ is context stable at one interior gate, it is context stable at every interior gate.
\end{corollary}

A harsher bar raises the \emph{value} of the budget but cannot change whether a finite budget exists: stability is a property of the model, not of the measurement standard.

\begin{theorem}[Quantitative tail criterion]\label{thm:tailcrit}
Let $w$ be positive and summable, $k \ge 1$, and suppose $\sum_{i \ge k} w_i \le (1-\tau) w_0$. Then $k^*_w(n,\tau) \le k$ for every $n \ge 1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
The hypothesis forces $\tau \le 1$. For $n \le k$, $R_w(n,k)=1\ge\tau$. For $n>k$,
\[
H_w(n)-H_w(k) \le \sum_{i\ge k} w_i \le (1-\tau)w_0 \le (1-\tau)H_w(k),
\]
using $w_0 = H_w(1) \le H_w(k)$. Hence $H_w(n) \le (2-\tau)H_w(k)$ and $R_w(n,k) \ge 1/(2-\tau) \ge \tau$, the last step being equivalent to $(1-\tau)^2 \ge 0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Zipf phase transition]\label{thm:zipf}
Let $w_i = (i+1)^{-s}$ and $0<\tau<1$. Then $w$ is context stable at $\tau$ if and only if $s>1$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
By Theorem~\ref{thm:summable}, stability is summability of $\sum_i (i+1)^{-s}$, which holds precisely for $s>1$ by the $p$-series criterion.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
If $s \le 1$, then for every candidate budget $K$ there is a context length $n$ with $k^*(n) > K$.
\end{corollary}

The critical exponent is exactly $1$: above it a bounded budget exists at every gate; at or below it the budget diverges with the context. Measuring the knee at two context lengths locates the model on one side of the transition.

\section{Merging heads: a max law}

Unnormalised attention masses add, so a merged head is the pointwise sum $w_1 + w_2$.

\begin{theorem}[Mediant inequality]\label{thm:mediant}
For positive profiles $w_1,w_2$, $n\ge1$ and any $k$,
\[
\min\big(R_{w_1}(n,k),R_{w_2}(n,k)\big) \le R_{w_1+w_2}(n,k) \le \max\big(R_{w_1}(n,k),R_{w_2}(n,k)\big).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Head mass is additive, so the merged value is the mediant $(A_1+A_2)/(B_1+B_2)$ with $B_1,B_2>0$. If $m$ is the minimum then $mB_j \le A_j$; summing gives $m(B_1+B_2)\le A_1+A_2$. The upper bound is symmetric.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Knee sandwich]\label{thm:sandwich}
For positive $w_1,w_2$, $n\ge1$, $\tau\le1$,
\[
\min\big(k^*_{w_1}(n,\tau),k^*_{w_2}(n,\tau)\big) \le k^*_{w_1+w_2}(n,\tau) \le \max\big(k^*_{w_1}(n,\tau),k^*_{w_2}(n,\tau)\big).
\]
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Upper: with $K$ the maximum of the two knees, each head passes at $K$ by monotonicity, so $\tau \le \min(R_{w_1}(n,K),R_{w_2}(n,K)) \le R_{w_1+w_2}(n,K)$ by Theorem~\ref{thm:mediant}. Lower: if $k$ is the merged knee, it passes, so by the upper mediant bound one head passes at $k$ and its knee is $\le k$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
Context stability is closed under mixing, with budget $\max(K_1,K_2)$. Conversely, a mixture containing a gapless head is not context stable, since summability is destroyed by a divergent summand (Corollary~\ref{cor:flatunstable}).
\end{corollary}

Stability obeys a max law, not a sum law: adding well-behaved heads never destabilises a model, while one heavy-tailed head sets the budget for the whole system. A testable consequence is that per-head knees measured separately should bracket the model-level knee.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Knee by bisection.} Monotonicity of $R_w(n,\cdot)$ makes ``budget $k$ passes'' a monotone predicate, so the knee is found by binary search on $[0,n]$ in $O(\log n)$ predicate evaluations after $O(n)$ prefix-sum precomputation; correctness is Theorem~\ref{thm:razor}, since bisection maintains a fail/pass bracket containing $k^*$.

\paragraph{Universal budget from a decay ratio.} Return $K(r,\tau)$ of Theorem~\ref{thm:closedform} in $O(1)$; it is valid at every context length.

\paragraph{Decay-ratio inversion.} Given a measured universal budget $K$ and gate $\tau$, return the largest $r$ with $r^K \le (1-\tau)(1-r)$ by bisection on $(0,1)$: the left side increases in $r$, the right side decreases. This converts a knee measurement into a bound on the internal decay ratio.

\section{Discussion}

\subsection{What the measurements establish}

Theorem~\ref{thm:razor} gives $12 < k^*(1024) \le 16$ at gate $0.98$, and Proposition~\ref{prop:plateau} certifies that the sub-knee grid strictly increases, so each failing point is independent evidence. The residual uncertainty is grid spacing. Conversely, the chain $\{16,16\}$ across a doubling of context cannot be upgraded to an equality law, since exact flatness fails even for ideal geometric decay (Theorem~\ref{thm:flatfalse}); what it supports is uniform boundedness.

\subsection{What separates the chains}

By Theorems~\ref{thm:summable} and~\ref{thm:zipf}, context stability is a summability property of the sorted spectrum. A rising chain signals a slowly decaying, critically or subcritically heavy tail, whose budget grows like $\Theta(n)$ in the gapless extreme (Theorem~\ref{thm:linear}); a flat chain signals a summable profile. Model scale enters only through its effect on the spectrum, and the sharp observable is the context sensitivity $\Delta(n)$ rather than the knee itself.

\subsection{A numerical consistency check}

Fitting a Zipf profile to the measured retention grid at context $1024$ (solving $R_{\mathrm{zipf}(s)}(1024,k)$ equal to the measured value at each $k$) yields exponents $2.35$, $2.30$, $2.29$, $2.24$ at $k=4,6,8,12$: stable to a few percent, and comfortably supercritical. For $s \in [2.24,2.35]$ the computed knee at gate $0.98$ is $11$--$15$ and does not move as $n$ grows from $1024$ to $65{,}536$, a falsifiable prediction of continued flatness. These figures are a numerical fit, not a proved statement; the proved statements are Theorems~\ref{thm:summable} and~\ref{thm:zipf}.

\subsection{Limitations}

The theory is model-free by design. It says nothing about why a spectrum decays as it does, nor about the task loss induced by truncation---retained mass is a proxy for quality, not quality itself. All statements assume positive weights in already-sorted order, whereas a real head's ranking varies by query and position, so an empirical knee aggregates over a distribution of profiles. Finally, measured inputs enter as hypotheses: Corollary~\ref{cor:measured} is conditional on the two measured numbers.

\subsection{Future work}

The sharpest open question is quantitative: for an asymptotically Zipf profile with $s>1$, is the knee at gate $\tau$ of size $\Theta\big((1-\tau)^{-1/(s-1)}\big)$ uniformly in $n$? The upper direction should follow from Theorem~\ref{thm:tailcrit}, which converts gate slack into a tail-mass budget; a matching lower bound by integral comparison would make two knee measurements at different gates determine $s$, turning a knee sweep into a spectrometer. Adjacent directions: exact head-count invariance of the merged budget; the mixed critical case where a subcritical head is diluted by supercritical ones; and finite-context quantification of how large $n$ must be before divergence at $s \le 1$ becomes visible.

